content.text:
## Coverage vs plan

Covered all 6 assigned items: chip-pagination math, CLEAR ALL / category-chip navigation logic, buffered-vs-applied state (including the scope-creep note), edge cases (zero-results, clear-all, rapid double-toggle), `useEffect`/functional-updater React-logic audit, and the tsc/vitest runs. Traced everything against the real file at PR head (worktree confirmed at `15dbd98e`), diffed against the true merge-base `2510cea5`. Found 3 must-fix and 2 should-fix defects, all confirmed either by an actual (now-deleted) test run or a pure-arithmetic simulation — not just read-and-assume.

## Findings

**🔴 1. `handleClearAll` sets `pendingCategoryId` to `undefined` instead of the real applied category — CLEAR+APPLY silently navigates the shopper up a category level.**
`overrides/app/pages/product-list/index.tsx:454-457`:
```js
const handleClearAll = () => {
  clearAll();
  setPendingCategoryId(undefined);
};
```
`applyFilters` (lines 566-586) treats `pendingCategoryId === undefined` (with a parent category present) as `categoryCleared` → navigates to `/category/${parentCategoryId}`, indistinguishable from the shopper explicitly removing the category chip. But CLEAR is meant only to reset the *pending draft* — the comment immediately above (449-453) even says the goal is so "a shopper ... would still see that category selected," yet the value chosen (`undefined`) means "no category / go to parent," not "keep showing what's actually applied." Correct value is `params.categoryId` (which the layout-effect resync already uses on open, and which is itself `undefined` on a non-category route — so this isn't just "always pass categoryId", it's "pass whatever the resync would produce"). Violates `useFilterDrawerState.ts`'s own docblock invariant (lines 26-31): a revert "must also restore whatever refine is actually still applied" — this extends to category, and index.tsx's CLEAR wiring breaks it.
CONFIRMED (full trace, code as written). ONE-OFF site, but shares a conceptual root with Finding 3 below (see synthesis).

**🔴 2. `handleCategoryChange`'s revert path (A→B→A) never restores `pendingRefine` — the code's own safety comment is wrong.**
`overrides/app/pages/product-list/index.tsx:442-447`:
```js
const handleCategoryChange = (newCategoryId: string) => {
  setPendingCategoryId(newCategoryId);
  if (newCategoryId !== params.categoryId) {
    clearAll();
  }
};
```
The comment block above it (431-441) explicitly claims: *"Reverting back to the current category restores whatever the URL already has applied (resync effect handles that on the next open)."* This is false — the drawer never closes during an in-drawer A→B→A radio round trip, so the `useLayoutEffect` (deps=`[isOpen]` only) never re-fires. I reproduced this directly against the real hook (throwaway test, deleted after running): seed applied state `{categoryId: 'dresses', c_refinementColor: ['Black']}`, switch pending category to `'tops'` (clears `pendingRefine` per the `!==` branch), switch back to `'dresses'` (`===` branch, no `clearAll()`, and nothing else resyncs) → `pendingRefine` stays `{}` instead of restoring to `{c_refinementColor: ['Black']}`. Pressing APPLY at that point silently wipes a real, currently-applied refinement the shopper never touched. Contrast with the category-chip's own `onRemove` (lines 606-609), which correctly pairs `setPendingCategoryId(undefined)` with `resyncPendingRefine(searchParams.refine)` — `handleCategoryChange`'s revert branch has no equivalent call.
CONFIRMED via test (output: expected `{c_refinementColor:['Black']}`, got `{}`). ONE-OFF site, same conceptual gap as Finding 1 (see synthesis).

**🔴 3. `useFilterDrawerState`'s resync effect is keyed on `isOpen` alone — any applied-filter change that reaches the component while the drawer stays open is never synced into the pending buffer.**
`overrides/app/pages/product-list/useFilterDrawerState.ts:79-87`. The comment justifies excluding `categoryId`/`selectedRefinements` from the deps solely to avoid the effect "stomping" a just-applied APPLY navigation — and that specific reasoning is sound (`applyFilters` calls `filterDrawer.onClose()` before `navigate()`, so `isOpen` is already `false` by the time the URL updates). But the same exclusion also means **any other** way `categoryId`/`selectedRefinements` can change while `isOpen` stays `true` is silently ignored. Concretely reachable via browser back/forward: user opens the drawer on `/category/dresses`, checks a colour, then presses the browser Back button (no `isOpen` transition involved at all) — the URL/params change to the previous category, but `pendingCategoryId`/`pendingRefine` stay stuck on the old category's values. I reproduced this directly against the real hook (throwaway test, deleted after running): `rerender({categoryId: 'clothing', ...})` with `isOpen` held `true` throughout leaves `pendingCategoryId` at the stale `'dresses'`. If APPLY is then pressed, `categoryChanged` fires and silently navigates the shopper back to `dresses`, undoing their own back-navigation.
A second, less-certain trigger: `ZimmDrawer` is invoked with `hasOverlay={false}` (`index.tsx:1172`; `zimmDrawer/index.tsx:59-62,223` confirms this genuinely omits `<DrawerOverlay>`, whose own doc comment says it's for drawers where the shopper "still needs to see" the page behind it) — if that also means the page-level chip bar/`handleChipClearAll` remain clickable while the drawer is open, that's a second concrete trigger for the identical staleness. I did **not** verify actual pointer-event pass-through (CSS/browser-specific, out of my lane) — flagging as PLAUSIBLE only; the back/forward trigger above needs no such assumption and is fully CONFIRMED on its own.
No existing test in `useFilterDrawerState.test.ts` exercises "props change while `isOpen` stays `true`" at all — this is a real blind spot, not a covered-and-accepted case.

**🟡 4. `handleBufferedToggle`'s multi-select "add" branch races on rapid double-toggle — one of two values can be silently dropped.**
`overrides/app/pages/product-list/index.tsx:493-497`:
```js
} else {
  setValues(attributeId, [...(pendingRefine[attributeId] ?? []), stringValue]);
}
```
This reads `pendingRefine` from the component's render-scope closure, not from `setPendingRefine`'s own `prev` updater — even though `setValues` internally *is* a correct functional updater (verified: `useFilterDrawerState.ts:93-100` uses `(prev) => ({...prev, [attributeId]: values})`). The bug is in the caller precomputing the full next array outside that updater. I reproduced this directly (throwaway test, deleted after running): two same-tick calls — `setValues('c_refinementColor', [...stale, 'Black'])` then `setValues('c_refinementColor', [...stale, 'White'])`, both reading the identical stale snapshot — leave `pendingRefine.c_refinementColor` as `['White']` only; `'Black'` is silently lost. I also checked the sibling single-select (`allowMultiple=false`) branch (lines 483-491): it has the same "read from outer scope" shape, but since it *replaces* (not merges) the array, last-call-wins is actually the objectively correct outcome for a radio-style facet — no bug there. The hook's own primitives (`setValues`/`removeValue`) are correctly written; this is a composition bug in index.tsx's caller only.
CONFIRMED via test. ONE-OFF site (multi-select add path only), but the underlying pattern ("read pending state from render scope right before calling a functional-updater setter") is the kind of shape that would repeat if more callers are added the same way.

**🟡 5. `SelectedChipRow`'s post-remove offset correction ignores `chipsPerPage` — only actually works at the mobile breakpoint.**
`overrides/app/pages/product-list/index.tsx:216-219`:
```js
onRemove={() => {
  chip.onRemove();
  if (offset > 0 && offset >= chips.length - 1) {
    onOffsetChange(offset - 1);
  }
}}
```
`SelectedChipRowProps` (174-184) never receives `chipsPerPage`, so this threshold can't be computed correctly — it happens to work only when `chipsPerPage === 1` (mobile: `chips.length - 1 === chips.length - chipsPerPage`). At `lg`/`xl` (2 or 3 per page), the correct threshold needs `- chipsPerPage`, which is missing. The separately-derived `safeChipOffset` (`Math.min(chipOffset, Math.max(0, pageChips.length - chipsPerPage))`, line 898) masks this on the *same* render (display always looks right immediately after a removal), but the raw, un-clamped `chipOffset` **state** itself is never corrected. I confirmed the concrete failure with a pure-arithmetic simulation of the exact formulas: 4 chips → page right (offset 1) → remove a chip (decrement condition false, raw `chipOffset` stays 1; display looks fine via the clamp) → add a new filter without touching the arrows (list back to 4 chips) → window silently jumps to offset 1, hiding chip 0, with no user action requesting it. Core windowing math itself (`safeChipOffset`/slice/`canChipLeft`/`canChipRight`) is correct — verified no off-by-one at the exact 3-vs-4-chip desktop boundary; the bug is isolated to this one correction branch.
CONFIRMED via simulation. ONE-OFF, root cause is the missing `chipsPerPage` prop.

**🔵 6. Note — chip-per-breakpoint counts have no in-code spec citation.** `chipsPerPage: number = useBreakpointValue({ base: 1, lg: 2, xl: 3 }) ?? 3` (line 897) — the adjacent comment only explains the type annotation, not why 1/2/3 map to those specific Chakra breakpoints. Can't confirm "correct against spec" from the code alone; deferring the actual count/breakpoint adjudication to the Figma/Playwright-layout lane.

**🔵 7. Note — `handleDrawerClose`'s reset (lines 462-466) is likely redundant** with the open-time resync (next open re-syncs regardless), but depending on Chakra's close-animation unmount timing (unverified, browser-dependent) could theoretically flash a blanked category selection during the close transition. Low confidence, noted for completeness only.

**Scope-creep observation (factual, not adjudicating):** the ticket's own scope note frames CLEAR/APPLY as *button rendering* for SFCC-847 with *behavior* deferred to SFCC-108 (blocked). What's actually shipped in `useFilterDrawerState.ts` (open-resync, `setValues`/`removeValue`/`clearAll`, `resyncPendingRefine`, and `buildAppliedRefine`-driven atomic commit) plus index.tsx's `handleClearAll`/`applyFilters` wiring is a complete, real buffered commit/discard implementation, not just rendering — and Findings 1-3 above are bugs *in that behavior*.

**Synthesis — Findings 1 & 2 share a root conflation:** `pendingCategoryId === undefined` is used to mean two different things across three call sites — "explicitly remove the category filter, go to parent" (the chip's own `onRemove`, which correctly pairs it with `resyncPendingRefine`) vs. "no pending override, defer to whatever's applied" (what CLEAR and a reverted radio-switch actually need). Only the chip-removal site handles this correctly; the other two don't, which is why both bugs independently surface as "APPLY does something the shopper didn't ask for."

## What I tried to falsify

- Hook's own `setValues`/`removeValue` functional-updater correctness — confirmed safe (real `prev` used, not closure state).
- Single-select/radio branch of `handleBufferedToggle` for the same race as Finding 4 — confirmed *not* buggy (replace semantics make last-call-wins correct there).
- Core chip-window slice math for an off-by-one at exactly 3 vs. 4 chips — confirmed correct.
- `pendingCategoryChanged`/`activeRefinements` fallback-to-empty during the secondary category prefetch — confirmed intentional/documented, not a bug.
- `useEffect` deps for the category-options-persistence effect (`[category]`, line 367) and scroll-reset effect (`[isRefetching]`, line 389) — both complete, no staleness.
- Einstein/DataCloud/ActiveData tracking effect (`[productSearchResult]` only, line 836) — traced through `keepPreviousData: true` semantics; not stale, since `isSearch`/`searchQuery`/`category`/`searchParams` are recomputed synchronously in the same render that produces any new `productSearchResult` reference.
- Zero-results and "all filters cleared" paths — no distinct state-logic defect beyond Findings 1-2.
- Ran the existing 10-test suite and `tsc --noEmit` to make sure my findings aren't already caught or contradicted.

## What I could not verify

- Whether `hasOverlay={false}` actually permits real pointer-event click-through to the page beneath the drawer (Finding 3's second trigger) — needs live DOM/CSS inspection, outside my lane; the browser-back trigger for the same finding needs no such assumption and stands on its own.
- Chakra's exact close-animation/unmount timing for Note 7 — browser-dependent, not verified.
- Did not run the full repo test/Playwright/BrowserStack suite — only `useFilterDrawerState.test.ts` (10/10 pass) and `tsc --noEmit` (clean, no output) from `/tmp/claude-501/pr117-review`, per my assigned scope.
- Did not review `ZimmFiltersDrawerContent`/`ZimmCheckboxRefinements`/`ZimmSizeRefinements` internals — out of my 3-file scope; if their `toggleFilter` wiring debounces or batches differently than I assumed, Finding 4's real-world trigger shape could differ (though the underlying code defect is present regardless of how it's triggered).

All three throwaway verification tests (`__scratch_staleClosure.test.ts`, `__scratch_race.test.ts`, `__scratch_categoryRevert.test.ts`) and the arithmetic simulation script were deleted after running; `git status --porcelain` on the worktree confirms no stray files remain under `overrides/`.
